--- Run 15's probe evening, 2026-08-17 (chronology; the findings are on the page)

The capability that made all of it possible: micro.cabal compiles with
-rtsopts, so `+RTS -A<n>` overrides the baked RTS line and a nursery sweep runs
on ONE binary. The page had been saying run-major.sh cannot give one binary two
RTS configurations -- true of the driver, and it had been read as true of the
binary. Two runs spent a build and a gate each to approximate what a `+RTS`
argument does for free.

Order of discovery, since it matters for how much was wasted: the ladder probe
came first and reproduced Run 14's 14.1/22.3 as a known-answer control, which
is the only reason the -A32m figure beside it could be trusted at once. The
collector mechanism fell out of the -s output that probe was already printing.

The shim refutation was an accident of a control: I rebuilt with the old shim
expecting to isolate it, got a binary that did not reproduce Run 14's md5
either, and only then thought to rebuild with the CURRENT shim -- which gave
the same md5 as the old-shim build. That control should have been first; it is
the cheaper of the two and it is the one that decides.

Four figures I published earlier in the day and then had to correct: the
five-shape gain attributed to the position term (it is the collector), the md5
difference attributed to the shim (it is a dependency relink), the "shape-
structural, therefore the ladder" argument (a general allocator gain would not
show on all 24 either, because the collector saving does not), and offtab's
