--- Run 15, third probe round, 2026-08-17

The ladder hits `list` and nothing else: +15.0% against +0.5%, -0.1%, -0.2%
on three strategy arms of the same shape in the same processes. Nobody had
asked, in two runs of arguing about the term, whether it touches the arms --
and it is the answer that matters, every published figure being a ratio to
`list`.

A model I built and then had to kill in one call. The poison leaves ~22 MB
retained and the victim's phase runs 82 majors against 1; the arithmetic
looked like it would close (82 x 22 MB x 0.42 ms/MB is the right order); and
it does not, because majors do not copy large objects. Extra copying is 7 MB
total, predicting 3 ms against ~650 ms observed. GC time 0.043s vs 0.059s
settles it: the term is mutator time, where Run 14 had already put it.

The lesson is the page's own: two instruments disagreeing is the finding, and
the cheap direct split (MUT vs GC) should have come before the model, not
after it.

--- Run 15, the checker's third pass, 2026-08-17

It found fifteen, and the first retracts a chain. Criterion runs benches in
ROSTER order, not in the order the -m glob patterns are given. The roster puts
the six conv shapes at positions 1-6 and the victim at 7, so a scan pairing
each candidate with the victim tested only the six that CAN precede it; the
other seventeen processes ran the victim first, and those "innocent" readings
are victim-alone baselines. The tell was on the page and I read past it: all
seventeen sat just BELOW the alone figure instead of scattering around it.

So the identity-permutation clue, the conv1d-24 pairing and a three-condition
rule that separated all 23 with no mismatch were all confounded with roster
position -- and the rule fitted perfectly because positions 1-6 ARE that set.
Run 14 has the same defect and its own notes assert the victim is 24th and
last, where it is 7th.

Four more came from reading the wrong phase of a two-bench -S trace: 82 majors
against 1 (it is 96 against 101, and the poison REMOVES majors), 22 MB left by
the poison (it is the victim's own live set, there without one), and 13 KB per
major (72.9 KB in the poisoned process; 13 KB was the other file).

What I did right, and it is the only reason the victim-set experiment survives:
that script PRINTED which bench ran first and I checked it. The poison scan did
not print it. Same session, same hazard, one instrumented and one not.
